      <title>Learning the oldest programming language (2024)</title>
      <link>https://uncenter.dev/posts/learning-fortran/</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Fortran has had several subsequent releases since then; FORTRAN 77, Fortran 90, Fortran 95, Fortran 2003, Fortran 2008, and the latest Fortran 2018. Which version of Fortran. Every Fortran program begins with program &lt;program-name&gt; and ends with end program &lt;program-name&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46301696"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;HN Discussion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href='https://uncenter.dev/posts/learning-fortran/'&gt;Read Original Article&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Top 3 Comments:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;dizzant:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;&amp;gt; a quick introduction to a few modern Fortran features: declaring variables, printing and reading to and from the terminal, if and select case, and stop&lt;p&gt;Pretty much sums up this one. Can&amp;#x27;t say that I agree if&amp;#x2F;select&amp;#x2F;stop are &amp;quot;modern&amp;quot; features.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;turing_complete:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;I was expecting Plankalkül: &lt;a href="https:&amp;#x2F;&amp;#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&amp;#x2F;wiki&amp;#x2F;Plankalk%C3%BCl" rel="nofollow"&gt;https:&amp;#x2F;&amp;#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&amp;#x2F;wiki&amp;#x2F;Plankalk%C3%BCl&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ginko:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;I expected this to be about Plankalkül or something devised by Babbage.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
